148
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,919
Let's begin with one of the most basic

149
00:05:41,759 --> 00:05:47,440
and widely used quality measures, the

150
00:05:43,919 --> 00:05:49,520
mean squared error or MSE.

151
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:51,600
Suppose we have two images. The true

152
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:53,360
image which we'll call Y and the

153
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:56,000
reconstructed image which we'll call

154
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:59,919
Yhat. Each image is made up of many

155
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,960
pixels indexed by I. If the image is 512

156
00:05:59,919 --> 00:06:06,520
x 512, then the total number of pixels n

157
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:06,520
is over 260,000.

158
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:13,280
The formula for MSE is simple. MSE= 1 /n

159
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:15,840
* the sum from i = 1 to n of the

160
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:17,520
difference between y sub i and y sub i

161
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:19,280
squar.

162
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:22,240
In words, this means we compare the two

163
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:24,160
images pixel by pixel. At each pixel, we

164
00:06:22,240 --> 00:06:26,000
take the difference, square it so that

165
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:28,319
positive and negative errors don't

166
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,880
cancel out, and then average over all

167
00:06:28,319 --> 00:06:33,360
pixels. That gives us the mean squared

168
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:35,520
error.

169
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:38,000
Now, let's go a bit deeper. When we

170
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:41,280
estimate a parameter, say theta, we

171
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,440
often write the estimate as theta hat.

172
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:45,440
The error between theta hat and the true

173
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:48,000
value theta can be analyzed in

174
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:50,560
expectation, meaning averaged over many

175
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,240
trials. When you expand the algebra, you

176
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:55,360
find that the mean squared error

177
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:57,600
naturally splits into two parts.

178
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:58,960
The first part is the variance. This

179
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:01,199
tells us how much our estimates

180
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,880
fluctuate around their average value.

181
00:07:01,199 --> 00:07:05,199
You can think of variance as a measure

182
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:07,520
of random scatter.

183
00:07:05,199 --> 00:07:09,199
The second part is the bias squared.

184
00:07:07,520 --> 00:07:11,120
This measures the difference between the

185
00:07:09,199 --> 00:07:13,360
average of our estimates and the true

186
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:17,520
value. If our method consistently

187
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:19,919
overshoots or undershoots, that's bias.

188
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:22,560
So in summary, MSE equals variance plus

189
00:07:19,919 --> 00:07:24,720
bias squared. Variance captures random

190
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:26,639
error, bias captures systematic error,

191
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:29,520
and together they define the total

192
00:07:26,639 --> 00:07:32,080
error. This decomposition is very

193
00:07:29,520 --> 00:07:34,160
useful. It reminds us that an algorithm

194
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,880
might have low variance but high bias or

195
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:38,960
vice versa. Understanding both helps us

196
00:07:36,880 --> 00:07:43,160
judge the quality of an estimator or an

197
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:43,160
image reconstruction method.

198
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:48,720
Now the mean squared error is not the

199
00:07:46,639 --> 00:07:50,560
only way to measure differences. There

200
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,720
are several variants each with slightly

201
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,479
different properties.

202
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:57,280
The first which we've already discussed

203
00:07:54,479 --> 00:07:58,639
is the mean squared error or MSE. This

204
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:00,240
is the average of the squared

205
00:07:58,639 --> 00:08:01,840
differences between prediction and

206
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:03,759
truth.

207
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:06,960
A closely related measure is the root

208
00:08:03,759 --> 00:08:08,720
mean squared error or RMSSE.

209
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:12,160
Here we simply take the square root of

210
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:13,840
the mean squared error. Why?

211
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,199
because this brings the units back to

212
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:17,120
the same scale as the original

213
00:08:15,199 --> 00:08:19,440
measurement.

214
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:22,560
For example, if we are measuring pixel

215
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:24,720
intensities, RMSE will be expressed in

216
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:27,759
the same units as those intensities,

217
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:29,759
which makes it easier to interpret.

218
00:08:27,759 --> 00:08:32,080
One important property of squaring is

219
00:08:29,759 --> 00:08:35,039
that it emphasizes larger errors much

220
00:08:32,080 --> 00:08:37,440
more strongly. If a difference is 100,

221
00:08:35,039 --> 00:08:39,919
squaring turns it into 10,000. That

222
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:42,240
means MSE and RMSSE heavily penalize

223
00:08:39,919 --> 00:08:43,919
large deviations.

224
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:46,560
Sometimes we want a measure that treats

225
00:08:43,919 --> 00:08:48,560
all errors more equally.

226
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:51,200
That's where the mean absolute error or

227
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:52,640
MAE comes in. Instead of squaring, we

228
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:55,040
take the absolute value of the

229
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:57,279
difference at each pixel than average.

230
00:08:55,040 --> 00:09:00,240
This is also called the L1 norm while

231
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:02,560
MSE is associated with the L2 norm. The

232
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:06,080
L1 norm is less sensitive to outliers

233
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:07,839
compared to the L2 norm.

234
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:10,399
Finally, we have the mean absolute

235
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:12,160
percentage error or MATE.

236
00:09:10,399 --> 00:09:14,000
This is the mean absolute error

237
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:16,560
expressed as a percentage of the true

238
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,080
value. In other words, it's may divided

239
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:21,440
by the ground truth at each point

240
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:23,920
multiplied by 100%. This can be useful

241
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:27,040
when we want to understand an error in

242
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:29,920
relative terms. For example, saying the

243
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:32,720
error is 5% rather than giving a raw

244
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:34,880
number.

245
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:38,080
So these different distance measures

246
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:40,320
MSE, RMSSE, ME and MATE give us

247
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:42,080
different perspectives on error. The

248
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:44,160
choice depends on the problem. Do we

249
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:45,839
want to penalize large errors more or do

250
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:48,839
we care more about relative error

251
00:09:45,839 --> 00:09:48,839
percentages?

252
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:55,040
So far these measures seem very

253
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:57,360
reasonable. Think about it this way. You

254
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:01,200
have one signal or one image and you

255
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:03,680
also have a standard, the ground truth.

256
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:05,040
By comparing them pixel by pixel, we're

257
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:06,959
essentially measuring the difference

258
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:09,680
between two curves or between two

259
00:10:06,959 --> 00:10:11,839
surfaces or even between two volumes in

260
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:13,760
three dimensions.

261
00:10:11,839 --> 00:10:15,680
The formula reduces to something very

262
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:17,839
intuitive. It's about the area between

263
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:19,920
the two curves. The yellow region you

264
00:10:17,839 --> 00:10:21,760
see here represents those differences.

265
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:23,279
The larger the area, the greater the

266
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:25,760
error.

267
00:10:23,279 --> 00:10:28,160
This is why MSE and related measures are

268
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:30,320
so widely used. They give us a direct

269
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:33,360
and interpretable way to say how close

270
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:35,120
or how far two images are. It's simple,

271
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:37,920
mathematically neat and visually

272
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:39,360
intuitive.

273
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:41,279
But here's an important point. While

274
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:43,279
this is a good first step, it is not the

275
00:10:41,279 --> 00:10:45,120
whole story.

276
00:10:43,279 --> 00:10:47,200
Measuring differences point by point

277
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:49,680
tells us something, but not everything.

278
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:52,160
In medical imaging, we also care about

279
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:55,200
structural similarity, system behavior,

280
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:56,640
and clinical tasks. So as we continue

281
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:59,640
you'll see that this is only the

282
00:10:56,640 --> 00:10:59,640
beginning.

283
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:04,880
Now let me briefly mention another type

284
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:07,360
of distance called information

285
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:10,480
divergence. This is where probability

286
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:12,320
theory comes in.

287
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:14,320
Suppose you don't just have two images

288
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:16,320
but instead you have two probability

289
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,880
distributions. For example, two

290
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:20,959
different histograms of pixel values.

291
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:22,480
The question becomes how do we measure

292
00:11:20,959 --> 00:11:25,480
the difference between these two

293
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:25,480
distributions?

294
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:30,079
One option is to use uklitian distance

295
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:31,839
just as before.

296
00:11:30,079 --> 00:11:34,240
But there is a more meaningful way in

297
00:11:31,839 --> 00:11:36,160
the context of information theory. This

298
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:39,200
is called the Kullback Liler divergence

299
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:42,079
or KL distance for short.

300
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:44,720
The formula looks a bit unusual. KL

301
00:11:42,079 --> 00:11:47,279
divergence equals the sum over X of P of

302
00:11:44,720 --> 00:11:49,760
X multiplied by the logarithm of P of X

303
00:11:47,279 --> 00:11:51,440
/ Q ofX.

304
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:53,839
You don't need to worry too much about

305
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:56,959
the details. This is beyond the scope of

306
00:11:53,839 --> 00:11:59,120
our lecture. But the idea is important.

307
00:11:56,959 --> 00:12:01,680
The KL divergence is always greater than

308
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,360
or equal to zero and it becomes exactly

309
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:06,399
zero if and only if the two

310
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:08,399
distributions are identical.

311
00:12:06,399 --> 00:12:11,279
One interesting property is that the KL

312
00:12:08,399 --> 00:12:13,200
divergence is not symmetric.

313
00:12:11,279 --> 00:12:14,959
In other words, the distance from P to Q

314
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:17,279
is not the same as the distance from Q

315
00:12:14,959 --> 00:12:19,600
to P. That may sound strange, but it has

316
00:12:17,279 --> 00:12:21,519
a good analogy. Think of climbing a

317
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:23,279
mountain. Going uphill is much harder

318
00:12:21,519 --> 00:12:25,920
than going back downhill, even though

319
00:12:23,279 --> 00:12:27,760
it's the same physical path. In the same

320
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:31,839
way, KL divergence measures

321
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:34,079
directionality in information.

322
00:12:31,839 --> 00:12:36,000
So, while we won't use this directly in

323
00:12:34,079 --> 00:12:38,880
our course, it's good to be aware that

324
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:40,639
such information-based distances exist.

325
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:42,800
They play a big role in areas like

326
00:12:40,639 --> 00:12:45,800
machine learning and statistical signal

327
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,800
processing.

328
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:51,120
Now just for your broader knowledge, let

329
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:53,279
me connect this to another important

330
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:55,360
concept in information theory. Mutual

331
00:12:53,279 --> 00:12:57,120
information.

332
00:12:55,360 --> 00:12:59,200
Mutual information is a way to measure

333
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:01,600
how much knowing one random variable

334
00:12:59,200 --> 00:13:03,040
tells us about another.

335
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:05,279
For example, suppose we have two

336
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:07,440
variables X and Y. If they are

337
00:13:05,279 --> 00:13:10,000
completely independent then measuring X

338
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:12,480
tells us nothing about Y. In that case

339
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:14,720
their mutual information is zero. On the

340
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:17,200
other hand if X and Y are perfectly

341
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:19,680
dependent meaning that once you know X

342
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:22,320
you completely know Y then their mutual

343
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:24,000
information is very high. Most real

344
00:13:22,320 --> 00:13:26,720
world situations fall somewhere in

345
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,560
between.

346
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:30,480
Mathematically mutual information can

347
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:33,040
actually be expressed in terms of the KL

348
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:34,399
divergence. Specifically, it's the KL

349
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:36,639
divergence between the joint

350
00:13:34,399 --> 00:13:39,200
distribution of X and Y and the product

351
00:13:36,639 --> 00:13:41,200
of their marginal distributions. Don't

352
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:43,519
worry about the details of the formula.

353
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:45,760
The key idea is that it quantifies how

354
00:13:43,519 --> 00:13:48,880
much information one variable provides

355
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:50,560
about the other.

356
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:52,560
You can also think of it this way. When

357
00:13:50,560 --> 00:13:54,240
you measure one variable, how much does

358
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:56,800
your uncertainty about the other

359
00:13:54,240 --> 00:13:58,880
variable decrease?

360
00:13:56,800 --> 00:14:02,079
That decrease in uncertainty is exactly

361
00:13:58,880 --> 00:14:04,160
what mutual information captures.

362
00:14:02,079 --> 00:14:06,079
In practice, this idea is very useful in

363
00:14:04,160 --> 00:14:08,480
areas like image registration where we

364
00:14:06,079 --> 00:14:10,160
align two images.

365
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:12,800
Instead of just matching pixel

366
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:15,279
intensities, we can maximize the mutual

367
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:17,040
information between the two images. That

368
00:14:15,279 --> 00:14:18,800
way, even if the images look very

369
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:20,959
different in terms of brightness or

370
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:24,199
contrast, we can still measure how well

371
00:14:20,959 --> 00:14:24,199
they correspond.

372
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:28,959
So, mutual information is essentially a

373
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:31,600
generalization of correlation. But in

374
00:14:28,959 --> 00:14:33,360
the language of information theory, it

375
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:35,279
goes beyond simple linear relationships

376
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:38,279
and captures any kind of statistical

377
00:14:35,279 --> 00:14:38,279
dependence.

378
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,519
Mutual information which we just

379
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:45,519
discussed is actually defined in terms

380
00:14:43,519 --> 00:14:48,399
of another very fundamental concept

381
00:14:45,519 --> 00:14:51,680
entropy.

382
00:14:48,399 --> 00:14:54,000
So what is entropy in this context?

383
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:55,920
Think of it as a measure of uncertainty.

384
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,079
If you have a probability distribution

385
00:14:55,920 --> 00:15:00,160
that is very spread out and uniform,

386
00:14:58,079 --> 00:15:01,600
then you have a lot of uncertainty. You

387
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:04,880
don't really know what the outcome will

388
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:07,120
be. That means the entropy is high.

389
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:09,600
On the other hand, if the distribution

390
00:15:07,120 --> 00:15:12,000
is sharply peaked like a delta function

391
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:14,320
where one outcome is guaranteed, then

392
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:17,519
there is no uncertainty. In that case,

393
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:19,839
the entropy is very low, even zero.

394
00:15:17,519 --> 00:15:21,839
So entropy tells us how much information

395
00:15:19,839 --> 00:15:24,399
or how much unpredictability is

396
00:15:21,839 --> 00:15:26,079
contained in a random variable. In

397
00:15:24,399 --> 00:15:28,240
information theory, this is a central

398
00:15:26,079 --> 00:15:31,680
concept because information itself is

399
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:33,680
really about reducing uncertainty.

400
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:35,600
Now mutual information can be written as

401
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:37,839
the difference between two entropies.

402
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:40,560
The entropy of Y by itself minus the

403
00:15:37,839 --> 00:15:42,800
entropy of Y given X. In other words, it

404
00:15:40,560 --> 00:15:45,360
measures how much uncertainty about Y is

405
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:47,440
reduced when you know X. That's exactly

406
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:49,440
what we mean by how much does X tell us

407
00:15:47,440 --> 00:15:51,199
about Y.

408
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:53,839
Again, you don't need to get bogged down

409
00:15:51,199 --> 00:15:56,320
in the formulas here. The key point is

410
00:15:53,839 --> 00:15:58,560
entropy captures uncertainty and mutual

411
00:15:56,320 --> 00:16:02,720
information measures how two variables

412
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:04,320
share or reduce that uncertainty.

413
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:06,320
For our purposes, I just want you to

414
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:08,639
know that these information theoretic

415
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:10,880
measures KL divergence, mutual

416
00:16:08,639 --> 00:16:12,480
information, and entropy are very

417
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:14,639
powerful, but they are more advanced

418
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:16,320
than what we need right now. Think of

419
00:16:14,639 --> 00:16:18,000
them as tools in the background which

420
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:20,480
complement the simpler measures like

421
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,320
mean squared error.

422
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:24,000
And with that, we will soon return to

423
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:25,839
the more practical measures that are

424
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:28,839
directly used in image quality

425
00:16:25,839 --> 00:16:28,839
assessment.

426
00:16:30,399 --> 00:16:35,600
Now let's see why mean squared error or

427
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:37,440
MSE is not always good enough.

428
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:39,519
At the top left we have the original

429
00:16:37,440 --> 00:16:42,320
image. The best version taken under

430
00:16:39,519 --> 00:16:44,160
ideal conditions. Below it you see five

431
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:46,800
different degraded versions of the same

432
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:49,279
image. Some look noisy, some are blurry

433
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:51,199
and some have other distortions. Clearly

434
00:16:49,279 --> 00:16:53,519
to the human eye these images do not

435
00:16:51,199 --> 00:16:55,519
look equally good.

436
00:16:53,519 --> 00:16:57,440
But here's the problem. When we compute

437
00:16:55,519 --> 00:16:59,199
the mean squared error between the

438
00:16:57,440 --> 00:17:02,160
original image and each of these five

439
00:16:59,199 --> 00:17:03,839
degraded ones, the result is the same

440
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:05,839
225.

441
00:17:03,839 --> 00:17:09,679
Mathematically, MSE tells us they are

442
00:17:05,839 --> 00:17:11,439
equally different from the original.

443
00:17:09,679 --> 00:17:14,079
Visually though, that's obviously not

444
00:17:11,439 --> 00:17:15,839
true. Some versions look much closer to

445
00:17:14,079 --> 00:17:18,160
the original while others look far

446
00:17:15,839 --> 00:17:21,360
worse. Our eyes immediately pick up

447
00:17:18,160 --> 00:17:23,520
those differences, but MSE cannot.

448
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:26,640
And this is the key point. MSE does not

449
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:28,720
reflect human perception very well. It

450
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:30,400
measures pixel-wise differences, but it

451
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:33,520
cannot capture whether the overall

452
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:35,120
structure of the image is preserved.

453
00:17:33,520 --> 00:17:37,200
This leads us to the next important

454
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:39,840
idea. We need a measure that better

455
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:42,400
matches what humans actually see. That's

456
00:17:39,840 --> 00:17:45,400
where structural similarity or SSM comes

457
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:45,400
in.

458
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:50,400
Now, let's think about this from the

459
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:52,640
perspective of the human visual system

460
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:54,559
or HVS.

461
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:56,799
The human eye does not look at images

462
00:17:54,559 --> 00:17:59,760
pixel by pixel the way mean squared

463
00:17:56,799 --> 00:18:01,919
error does. MSE simply compares each

464
00:17:59,760 --> 00:18:04,160
pixel individually, treats every error

465
00:18:01,919 --> 00:18:06,320
the same, and then adds them up. That's

466
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:10,400
a bottomup approach, starting from the

467
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:12,160
smallest units and working upward.

468
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:13,919
But our visual system works very

469
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:16,240
differently.

470
00:18:13,919 --> 00:18:18,320
We are much more sensitive to structural

471
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:20,000
information, the patterns, edges, and

472
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:22,640
relationships that give an image its

473
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,320
overall form. For example, even a small

474
00:18:22,640 --> 00:18:26,320
change in the background or a shift in

475
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:28,799
texture is something we can notice right

476
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:30,480
away. Our brains are highly adapted to

477
00:18:28,799 --> 00:18:32,320
detect these kinds of contextual

478
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:34,720
changes.

479
00:18:32,320 --> 00:18:36,320
In the classical view, the focus was on

480
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:38,799
error visibility. If you see a

481
00:18:36,320 --> 00:18:40,640
discrepancy, count it as an error. In

482
00:18:38,799 --> 00:18:42,720
the newer view, the focus shifts to

483
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:44,400
structural distortion. What matters is

484
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:46,400
whether the structure of the image has

485
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:48,320
been preserved.

486
00:18:46,400 --> 00:18:51,120
This also connects to the way our vision

487
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:53,360
system interprets images.

488
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:55,280
Rather than starting with tiny details,

489
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:57,760
many researchers argue that we first

490
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:59,840
process the global structure, sometimes

491
00:18:57,760 --> 00:19:01,840
described in terms of topological

492
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:03,760
features. These are high-level

493
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:05,600
properties such as whether objects are

494
00:19:03,760 --> 00:19:07,679
connected, how many distinct regions

495
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:10,080
there are, or whether certain shapes

496
00:19:07,679 --> 00:19:11,840
remain intact. Importantly, these

497
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:16,080
properties don't change if the image is

498
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:18,080
stretched, rotated, or rescaled.

499
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:20,160
So, here's the philosophical shift.

500
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:22,720
Instead of measuring differences pixel

501
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:24,559
by pixel, we want to measure how much of

502
00:19:22,720 --> 00:19:26,720
the structural information has been

503
00:19:24,559 --> 00:19:28,720
preserved. That is what structural

504
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:30,720
similarity is all about. And it's why it

505
00:19:28,720 --> 00:19:34,360
is far more aligned with how humans

506
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:34,360
actually see images.

507
00:19:35,919 --> 00:19:40,240
Here we arrive at what I like to call an

508
00:19:37,679 --> 00:19:43,039
instant classic. This is the landmark

509
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:45,679
2004 paper by Joe Wang, Alan Bovic,

510
00:19:43,039 --> 00:19:48,080
Hamid Shik, and Eero Simmonelli titled

511
00:19:45,679 --> 00:19:51,760
image quality assessment from error

512
00:19:48,080 --> 00:19:53,679
visibility to structural similarity.

513
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:56,160
Before this work, most image quality

514
00:19:53,679 --> 00:19:57,919
measures focused on error visibility,

515
00:19:56,160 --> 00:20:00,160
essentially counting differences pixel

516
00:19:57,919 --> 00:20:02,640
by pixel, the way mean squared error

517
00:20:00,160 --> 00:20:04,160
does. What this paper introduced was a

518
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:05,840
completely different perspective.

519
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:08,799
Instead of measuring errors, we should

520
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:10,640
measure structural similarity.

521
00:20:08,799 --> 00:20:12,960
The idea is built on the assumption that

522
00:20:10,640 --> 00:20:15,280
the human visual system is highly tuned

523
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:17,039
to extract structural information from

524
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:19,200
images.

525
00:20:17,039 --> 00:20:22,080
So rather than asking how many errors

526
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:23,520
can we see, the SSM framework asks how

527
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:26,400
well is the structure of the image

528
00:20:23,520 --> 00:20:28,240
preserved.

529
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:30,640
This shift in thinking turned out to be

530
00:20:28,240 --> 00:20:32,320
incredibly powerful.

531
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:34,720
The paper has been cited tens of

532
00:20:32,320 --> 00:20:36,880
thousands of times and SSIM quickly

533
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:38,720
became a standard tool not only in image

534
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:40,640
processing research but also in

535
00:20:38,720 --> 00:20:42,400
practical applications like video

536
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:44,559
compression, image restoration and

537
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:46,480
medical imaging.

538
00:20:44,559 --> 00:20:48,799
So from this point forward when we talk

539
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:50,559
about perceptual image quality we are

540
00:20:48,799 --> 00:20:53,960
really building on the foundation laid

541
00:20:50,559 --> 00:20:53,960
by this work.

542
00:20:55,280 --> 00:20:59,840
Now let's look at an example to see how

543
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:02,559
the structural similarity index or SSIM

544
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:04,320
actually works in practice.

545
00:21:02,559 --> 00:21:07,520
On the top left we have the reference

546
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:09,679
image the original undistorted version.

547
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:12,320
If we compare this image with itself the

548
00:21:09,679 --> 00:21:15,039
SSM score is exactly one. That makes

549
00:21:12,320 --> 00:21:16,799
sense because they are identical.

550
00:21:15,039 --> 00:21:20,320
Now compare the original with the two

551
00:21:16,799 --> 00:21:22,720
images next to it on the top row. Both

552
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:26,480
look quite similar to the original. one

553
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:28,400
produces an SSM value of 0.949 comma the

554
00:21:26,480 --> 00:21:30,240
other 0.989.

555
00:21:28,400 --> 00:21:32,000
These numbers are very close to one

556
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:35,039
reflecting the fact that the images are

557
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:36,880
almost identical to our eyes.

558
00:21:35,039 --> 00:21:39,039
On the bottom row, however, we see very

559
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:41,120
different results.

560
00:21:39,039 --> 00:21:43,039
One image has been heavily pixelated,

561
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:45,039
another blurred, and another corrupted

562
00:21:43,039 --> 00:21:47,520
with noise. When compared to the

563
00:21:45,039 --> 00:21:51,039
original, their SSM values drop

564
00:21:47,520 --> 00:21:55,200
significantly around 0.67,

565
00:21:51,039 --> 00:21:57,280
0.69. 69 comma and about 0.72.

566
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:59,919
That means they retain only about 2/3 of

567
00:21:57,280 --> 00:22:02,000
the structural similarity.

568
00:21:59,919 --> 00:22:04,159
The important thing here is that SSM

569
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,559
values line up with what we visually

570
00:22:04,159 --> 00:22:08,880
perceive. Images that look good to us

571
00:22:06,559 --> 00:22:11,679
score close to one. Images that look

572
00:22:08,880 --> 00:22:13,760
distorted or degraded score much lower.

573
00:22:11,679 --> 00:22:15,679
That is the power of SSIM. It bridges

574
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:19,240
the gap between mathematical measurement

575
00:22:15,679 --> 00:22:19,240
and human vision.

576
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:24,320
Now let's look at how the structural

577
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:26,159
similarity index or SSIM is actually

578
00:22:24,320 --> 00:22:28,000
computed.

579
00:22:26,159 --> 00:22:31,120
Suppose we have two images which we'll

580
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:32,559
call signal X and signal Y.

581
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:34,480
The first step is to look at their

582
00:22:32,559 --> 00:22:36,559
luminance which simply means the average

583
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:38,400
brightness level. We measure the mean

584
00:22:36,559 --> 00:22:41,440
intensity of each image and make sure we

585
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:43,760
are comparing them on the same scale.

586
00:22:41,440 --> 00:22:45,840
Next, we look at the contrast. This is

587
00:22:43,760 --> 00:22:47,840
captured by the standard deviation, how

588
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:50,320
much the pixel values vary around the

589
00:22:47,840 --> 00:22:52,159
mean. A high standard deviation means

590
00:22:50,320 --> 00:22:54,159
strong contrast, while a low standard

591
00:22:52,159 --> 00:22:56,480
deviation means the image looks flat or

592
00:22:54,159 --> 00:22:58,720
washed out. So, we compute the standard

593
00:22:56,480 --> 00:23:00,720
deviation for each image to quantify its

594
00:22:58,720 --> 00:23:02,799
contrast.

595
00:23:00,720 --> 00:23:04,799
After normalizing for luminance and

596
00:23:02,799 --> 00:23:07,440
contrast, we focus on the most important

597
00:23:04,799 --> 00:23:09,440
part, the structure. This step captures

598
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:11,600
how patterns of pixels in one image

599
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:14,240
relate to patterns in the other. Whether

600
00:23:11,600 --> 00:23:17,120
the edges, textures, and fine details

601
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:19,600
line up.

602
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,919
Finally, SSIM combines all three

603
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:24,159
comparisons, luminance, contrast, and

604
00:23:21,919 --> 00:23:26,720
structure into a single value between

605
00:23:24,159 --> 00:23:29,039
zero and one. A score of one means the

606
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:30,720
images are structurally identical. A

607
00:23:29,039 --> 00:23:33,200
lower score means important structural

608
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:35,760
information has been lost.

609
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:37,919
So in summary, SIM works by checking

610
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:39,760
three things. Do the images have the

611
00:23:37,919 --> 00:23:42,080
same brightness, the same contrast, and

612
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:44,080
the same structural patterns. When all

613
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:47,080
three align, we say the images are very

614
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:47,080
similar.

615
00:23:48,559 --> 00:23:51,520
As I mentioned, the structural

616
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:53,520
similarity index is built on three

617
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:57,039
components. Luminance, contrast, and

618
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:59,600
structure, or LC and S for short.

619
00:23:57,039 --> 00:24:01,280
Let's start with luminance.

620
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:03,679
Suppose we have an image X with n

621
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:05,760
pixels. To find its average brightness

622
00:24:03,679 --> 00:24:08,480
or mean, we simply add up all the pixel

623
00:24:05,760 --> 00:24:11,840
values and divide by n. This gives us

624
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:13,919
the mean value, which we call mu of x.

625
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:16,559
Next, we remove the mean by subtracting

626
00:24:13,919 --> 00:24:18,559
the mean of x from every pixel. This is

627
00:24:16,559 --> 00:24:20,480
the first step of normalization. It

628
00:24:18,559 --> 00:24:22,799
centers the image so that the average

629
00:24:20,480 --> 00:24:24,480
brightness is zero.

630
00:24:22,799 --> 00:24:27,360
Once we have the mean, we can also

631
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:29,360
compute the standard deviation.

632
00:24:27,360 --> 00:24:31,679
This measures how much the pixel values

633
00:24:29,360 --> 00:24:34,799
vary around the mean. Notice that the

634
00:24:31,679 --> 00:24:36,880
formula here uses 1 / n minus one, not

635
00:24:34,799 --> 00:24:38,960
just n. That detail comes from

636
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:42,880
statistics. It gives us an unbiased

637
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:44,880
estimate of the standard deviation.

638
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:46,960
Finally, we can normalize the image even

639
00:24:44,880 --> 00:24:49,440
further by dividing each pixel by the

640
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:51,600
standard deviation.

641
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:53,440
After this second step of normalization,

642
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:56,400
the transformed image will have a mean

643
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:58,240
of zero and a standard deviation of one.

644
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:59,919
In other words, it has been rescaled so

645
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:01,919
that brightness and contrast are

646
00:24:59,919 --> 00:25:04,240
standardized.

647
00:25:01,919 --> 00:25:06,720
We repeat this process for both images X

648
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:08,480
and Y. Only then do we compare them

649
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:10,320
first in terms of luminance, then

650
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:12,240
contrast, and finally their structural

651
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:14,320
relationship.

652
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:17,360
So these are the basic operations behind

653
00:25:14,320 --> 00:25:19,279
SSM. They are simple statistical tools,

654
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:21,520
but together they allow us to capture

655
00:25:19,279 --> 00:25:26,039
how similar two images are in terms of

656
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:26,039
brightness, contrast, and structure.

657
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:30,559
When we construct a similarity measure,

658
00:25:28,720 --> 00:25:32,400
the goal is to capture not just how

659
00:25:30,559 --> 00:25:35,679
different two images are, but how

660
00:25:32,400 --> 00:25:37,520
similar they are. To do this properly,

661
00:25:35,679 --> 00:25:40,640
we want our measure to satisfy three

662
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:43,200
basic conditions or postulates.

663
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:45,120
First, symmetry. The similarity between

664
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:47,600
X and Y should be the same as the

665
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:49,760
similarity between Y and X. In other

666
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:51,919
words, order doesn't matter. If you

667
00:25:49,760 --> 00:25:54,000
compare image A with image B, you should

668
00:25:51,919 --> 00:25:57,039
get the same result as comparing image B

669
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:59,520
with image A.

670
00:25:57,039 --> 00:26:01,679
Second, boundedness. The similarity

671
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:04,240
value should always be between zero and

672
00:26:01,679 --> 00:26:06,559
one. A score of one means the images are

673
00:26:04,240 --> 00:26:09,440
perfectly identical. A score closer to

674
00:26:06,559 --> 00:26:12,320
zero means they are very different.

675
00:26:09,440 --> 00:26:14,320
Third, a unique maximum. The similarity

676
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:16,480
should only reach the maximum value of

677
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:18,799
one if the two images are exactly the

678
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:20,559
same pixel for pixel. That way the

679
00:26:18,799 --> 00:26:22,640
measure has a clear and meaningful

680
00:26:20,559 --> 00:26:24,720
interpretation.

681
00:26:22,640 --> 00:26:27,120
These three conditions symmetry,

682
00:26:24,720 --> 00:26:29,360
boundedness, and unique maximum make

683
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:32,320
sure that a similarity measure behaves

684
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:36,600
logically and reliably. SSM is designed

685
00:26:32,320 --> 00:26:36,600
to satisfy all of these postulates.

686
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:43,600
So let's begin with the first component

687
00:26:39,840 --> 00:26:45,440
of SSM, the luminance comparison.

688
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:48,159
Remember luminance simply means the

689
00:26:45,440 --> 00:26:50,000
average brightness of an image.

690
00:26:48,159 --> 00:26:51,919
If two images have the same mean

691
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:54,559
intensity, they should score highly on

692
00:26:51,919 --> 00:26:56,480
luminance similarity. If one image is

693
00:26:54,559 --> 00:26:59,360
much brighter or darker than the other,

694
00:26:56,480 --> 00:27:01,360
the similarity should be lower.

695
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:03,760
Mathematically, the luminance comparison

696
00:27:01,360 --> 00:27:08,080
is written like this.

697
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:12,240
L of x and y = 2 * mux * muy plus a

698
00:27:08,080 --> 00:27:15,279
constant c1 all divided by mux^2 + muy 2

699
00:27:12,240 --> 00:27:17,840
plus that same constant c1.

700
00:27:15,279 --> 00:27:20,480
Here mux and muy represent the average

701
00:27:17,840 --> 00:27:23,039
brightness of images x and y. The

702
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:25,200
constant c1 is added to make the formula

703
00:27:23,039 --> 00:27:27,200
stable. Otherwise, if both means are

704
00:27:25,200 --> 00:27:30,960
very close to zero, the denominator

705
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:33,840
becomes tiny and the ratio unstable.

706
00:27:30,960 --> 00:27:36,240
To choose c1, we take the square of k1 *

707
00:27:33,840 --> 00:27:39,200
l, where l is the dynamic range of pixel

708
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:41,279
values. For an 8bit grayscale image, l

709
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:43,279
is 255.

710
00:27:41,279 --> 00:27:45,279
K1 is just a very small number, much

711
00:27:43,279 --> 00:27:47,840
less than one.

712
00:27:45,279 --> 00:27:49,840
Now notice a few important properties.

713
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:52,000
If the two means are equal, then the

714
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:54,480
numerator and denominator are the same.

715
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:57,360
So the luminance comparison equals 1,

716
00:27:54,480 --> 00:27:59,279
meaning perfect similarity.

717
00:27:57,360 --> 00:28:01,600
If the two means are very different, the

718
00:27:59,279 --> 00:28:03,760
numerator becomes small relative to the

719
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:05,360
denominator. So the luminance comparison

720
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:07,520
approaches zero, meaning poor

721
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:09,279
similarity.

722
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:11,840
This simple formula ensures that

723
00:28:09,279 --> 00:28:14,080
luminance similarity satisfies the three

724
00:28:11,840 --> 00:28:16,640
postulates we discussed earlier. It is

725
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:18,799
symmetric bounded between zero and one

726
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:20,960
and it reaches the maximum value of one

727
00:28:18,799 --> 00:28:23,200
only when the two images have identical

728
00:28:20,960 --> 00:28:24,880
mean brightness.

729
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:27,279
So luminance is the first step in

730
00:28:24,880 --> 00:28:31,320
measuring structural similarity. Next

731
00:28:27,279 --> 00:28:31,320
we'll look at contrast comparison.

732
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:36,559
Let's take a closer look at the

733
00:28:34,559 --> 00:28:40,080
luminance term and see why it behaves

734
00:28:36,559 --> 00:28:41,919
the way we want. Here we let mux the

735
00:28:40,080 --> 00:28:44,399
mean of image x be represented by the

736
00:28:41,919 --> 00:28:46,720
letter a and we let mui the mean of

737
00:28:44,399 --> 00:28:49,679
image y be represented by the variable

738
00:28:46,720 --> 00:28:51,919
x. Now the question is as x changes what

739
00:28:49,679 --> 00:28:54,640
value of x will maximize the luminance

740
00:28:51,919 --> 00:28:56,559
comparison.

741
00:28:54,640 --> 00:28:58,559
To answer this we take the formula for

742
00:28:56,559 --> 00:29:01,360
the luminance term and compute its first

743
00:28:58,559 --> 00:29:03,440
derivative with respect to x. By setting

744
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:04,799
that derivative equal to zero we can

745
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:07,360
find the point where the function

746
00:29:04,799 --> 00:29:09,200
reaches its maximum.

747
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:11,440
When we go through the algebra, the

748
00:29:09,200 --> 00:29:14,240
conclusion is very clear. The maximum

749
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:16,480
occurs when X equals A. In other words,

750
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:19,200
when the mean brightness of image Y is

751
00:29:16,480 --> 00:29:20,880
equal to the mean brightness of image X.

752
00:29:19,200 --> 00:29:22,799
At that point, the luminance comparison

753
00:29:20,880 --> 00:29:24,799
reaches its maximum possible value,

754
00:29:22,799 --> 00:29:27,039
which is one.

755
00:29:24,799 --> 00:29:28,799
This confirms the intuition. Two images

756
00:29:27,039 --> 00:29:30,559
will only be perfectly similar in

757
00:29:28,799 --> 00:29:33,200
luminance if their average brightness

758
00:29:30,559 --> 00:29:35,120
levels are the same. If one image is

759
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:36,799
brighter or darker than the other, the

760
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:39,600
luminance comparison will drop

761
00:29:36,799 --> 00:29:41,840
accordingly.

762
00:29:39,600 --> 00:29:44,320
So this mathematical exercise is really

763
00:29:41,840 --> 00:29:46,640
just a proof of what we already expect.

764
00:29:44,320 --> 00:29:48,240
The luminance term is maximized when the

765
00:29:46,640 --> 00:29:50,240
two images have the same mean

766
00:29:48,240 --> 00:29:51,919
brightness.

767
00:29:50,240 --> 00:29:54,240
That's the first aspect of structural

768
00:29:51,919 --> 00:29:58,600
similarity. Next, we'll move on to the

769
00:29:54,240 --> 00:29:58,600
second aspect, contrast comparison.

770
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:04,480
The second component of SSM is the

771
00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:06,559
contrast comparison.

772
00:30:04,480 --> 00:30:08,320
Even if two images have the same average

773
00:30:06,559 --> 00:30:10,080
brightness, they may still look very

774
00:30:08,320 --> 00:30:11,600
different if their contrast is not the

775
00:30:10,080 --> 00:30:13,600
same.

776
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:15,919
One might look sharp and vivid while the

777
00:30:13,600 --> 00:30:17,440
other looks flat or washed out. To

778
00:30:15,919 --> 00:30:19,919
capture this, we use the standard

779
00:30:17,440 --> 00:30:23,520
deviation of pixel values. Sigma X for

780
00:30:19,919 --> 00:30:25,360
image X and sigma Y for image Y.

781
00:30:23,520 --> 00:30:27,200
The formula for contrast comparison

782
00:30:25,360 --> 00:30:30,559
looks very similar to the one we used

783
00:30:27,200 --> 00:30:33,840
for luminance. It is written as C of X

784
00:30:30,559 --> 00:30:38,240
and Y equals 2 * sigma X * sigma Y plus

785
00:30:33,840 --> 00:30:41,279
a constant C2 / sigma X^ 2 + sigma Y^ 2

786
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:43,120
plus that same constant C2.

787
00:30:41,279 --> 00:30:44,880
Here again the constant is added to

788
00:30:43,120 --> 00:30:48,640
prevent instability when values are

789
00:30:44,880 --> 00:30:50,960
close to zero. C2 is defined as K2 * L^

790
00:30:48,640 --> 00:30:52,559
2 where L is the dynamic range of the

791
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:55,039
image.

792
00:30:52,559 --> 00:30:57,360
Now notice how this works. If the two

793
00:30:55,039 --> 00:30:59,279
images have the same standard deviation,

794
00:30:57,360 --> 00:31:01,679
meaning their contrast are the same,

795
00:30:59,279 --> 00:31:06,399
then the numerator and denominator match

796
00:31:01,679 --> 00:31:08,559
and the contrast comparison equals 1.

797
00:31:06,399 --> 00:31:10,640
If one image has much higher or lower

798
00:31:08,559 --> 00:31:12,480
contrast than the other, then the ratio

799
00:31:10,640 --> 00:31:14,320
becomes smaller and the similarity

800
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:16,240
drops.

801
00:31:14,320 --> 00:31:19,120
This design is also consistent with how

802
00:31:16,240 --> 00:31:21,200
the human visual system works.

803
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:22,880
For example, in a very dark room, small

804
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:25,200
changes in brightness can be quite

805
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:27,120
noticeable. But in a very bright, high

806
00:31:25,200 --> 00:31:29,440
contrast scene, the same small changes

807
00:31:27,120 --> 00:31:31,039
are much harder to see. The formula

808
00:31:29,440 --> 00:31:33,360
reflects this effect, sometimes called

809
00:31:31,039 --> 00:31:35,279
contrast masking.

810
00:31:33,360 --> 00:31:37,519
So contrast is the second pillar of

811
00:31:35,279 --> 00:31:39,279
structural similarity. It ensures that

812
00:31:37,519 --> 00:31:41,039
two images not only have the same

813
00:31:39,279 --> 00:31:44,399
average brightness but also the same

814
00:31:41,039 --> 00:31:46,000
level of variation around that average.

815
00:31:44,399 --> 00:31:49,640
Next, we'll look at the third pillar,

816
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:49,640
the structural comparison.

817
00:31:51,360 --> 00:31:55,679
Now, let's analyze the contrast term a

818
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:57,679
bit more carefully.

819
00:31:55,679 --> 00:31:59,360
The key idea here is that the human

820
00:31:57,679 --> 00:32:01,279
visual system does not respond to

821
00:31:59,360 --> 00:32:04,159
absolute changes in brightness, but

822
00:32:01,279 --> 00:32:05,840
rather to relative changes.

823
00:32:04,159 --> 00:32:08,080
This is exactly what Weber's law

824
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:10,159
describes. It states that the smallest

825
00:32:08,080 --> 00:32:11,919
noticeable change in a stimulus is

826
00:32:10,159 --> 00:32:14,000
proportional to the background level of

827
00:32:11,919 --> 00:32:15,679
that stimulus.

828
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:17,600
For example, if you are in a very dark

829
00:32:15,679 --> 00:32:19,919
room, even a tiny spot of light is

830
00:32:17,600 --> 00:32:22,240
noticeable right away. But if you are in

831
00:32:19,919 --> 00:32:24,399
a brightly lit room, the very same tiny

832
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:26,159
light would be invisible. The background

833
00:32:24,399 --> 00:32:28,480
intensity is so high that the small

834
00:32:26,159 --> 00:32:29,919
change is masked.

835
