appear in images but are not really

1383
00:53:33,280 --> 00:53:38,319
there. They are ghosts created by

1384
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:40,160
limitations or mismatches in the imaging

1385
00:53:38,319 --> 00:53:42,000
process.

1386
00:53:40,160 --> 00:53:44,160
For example, in CT, the motion of the

1387
00:53:42,000 --> 00:53:46,880
heart can create blurry or duplicated

1388
00:53:44,160 --> 00:53:48,640
structures. In ultrasound, echoes may

1389
00:53:46,880 --> 00:53:50,880
bounce back and forth and appear as

1390
00:53:48,640 --> 00:53:53,440
multiple spots even though only one

1391
00:53:50,880 --> 00:53:55,119
structure exists. Recognizing and

1392
00:53:53,440 --> 00:53:59,319
understanding artifacts is critical

1393
00:53:55,119 --> 00:53:59,319
because they can mislead interpretation.

1394
00:54:01,440 --> 00:54:05,760
Now let's look at a specific example of

1395
00:54:03,440 --> 00:54:08,240
artifacts. In this case, metal artifacts

1396
00:54:05,760 --> 00:54:10,079
in CT imaging.

1397
00:54:08,240 --> 00:54:12,559
Suppose a patient has a hip fracture and

1398
00:54:10,079 --> 00:54:14,640
receives metal implants.

1399
00:54:12,559 --> 00:54:16,400
When we take X-rays or CT scans, the

1400
00:54:14,640 --> 00:54:18,800
metal is so dense that it blocks or

1401
00:54:16,400 --> 00:54:20,480
severely distorts the X-ray beams. The

1402
00:54:18,800 --> 00:54:22,400
reconstruction algorithm, however,

1403
00:54:20,480 --> 00:54:25,359
assumes it has complete and accurate

1404
00:54:22,400 --> 00:54:27,119
data along every path. It doesn't know

1405
00:54:25,359 --> 00:54:29,200
that some of the information is missing

1406
00:54:27,119 --> 00:54:31,440
or corrupted.

1407
00:54:29,200 --> 00:54:33,920
As a result, the system produces streaks

1408
00:54:31,440 --> 00:54:36,000
and bands radiating from the metal.

1409
00:54:33,920 --> 00:54:38,640
These bright and dark lines are not real

1410
00:54:36,000 --> 00:54:40,720
anatomical structures. They are purely

1411
00:54:38,640 --> 00:54:43,599
computational artifacts caused by

1412
00:54:40,720 --> 00:54:45,200
missing or distorted data.

1413
00:54:43,599 --> 00:54:47,520
In the figure here, the first column

1414
00:54:45,200 --> 00:54:49,920
shows uncorrected CT images with severe

1415
00:54:47,520 --> 00:54:51,599
streak artifacts. The last column shows

1416
00:54:49,920 --> 00:54:53,680
the ground truth, what the anatomy

1417
00:54:51,599 --> 00:54:55,520
should look like.

1418
00:54:53,680 --> 00:54:58,319
Researchers have developed various ways

1419
00:54:55,520 --> 00:55:00,960
to reduce these artifacts.

1420
00:54:58,319 --> 00:55:03,119
One common method is NMAR or normalized

1421
00:55:00,960 --> 00:55:05,599
metal artifact reduction, which improves

1422
00:55:03,119 --> 00:55:07,520
the image somewhat. More recently, deep

1423
00:55:05,599 --> 00:55:09,520
learning methods such as convolutional

1424
00:55:07,520 --> 00:55:11,440
neural networks have been applied to

1425
00:55:09,520 --> 00:55:14,480
further clean up the image and recover

1426
00:55:11,440 --> 00:55:16,400
structures hidden by artifacts.

1427
00:55:14,480 --> 00:55:18,400
You can see that the CNN results are

1428
00:55:16,400 --> 00:55:21,440
closer to the ground truth with clearer

1429
00:55:18,400 --> 00:55:23,520
anatomy and fewer streaks.

1430
00:55:21,440 --> 00:55:25,440
The key point here is that artifacts are

1431
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:28,079
not real structures, but they can easily

1432
00:55:25,440 --> 00:55:30,160
confuse interpretation.

1433
00:55:28,079 --> 00:55:31,920
They depend on the imaging modality, the

1434
00:55:30,160 --> 00:55:34,000
reconstruction algorithm, and the

1435
00:55:31,920 --> 00:55:36,160
clinical situation. That's why

1436
00:55:34,000 --> 00:55:38,160
recognizing artifacts and knowing how to

1437
00:55:36,160 --> 00:55:41,160
reduce them is so important in medical

1438
00:55:38,160 --> 00:55:41,160
imaging.

1439
00:55:42,800 --> 00:55:47,920
So far we've covered general measures

1440
00:55:44,640 --> 00:55:50,160
like MSE, KL distance and SSIM and then

1441
00:55:47,920 --> 00:55:52,960
system specific measures such as noise

1442
00:55:50,160 --> 00:55:54,640
resolution and artifacts.

1443
00:55:52,960 --> 00:55:58,000
Now we move to the third and most

1444
00:55:54,640 --> 00:56:00,160
important part task specific measures.

1445
00:55:58,000 --> 00:56:01,839
When manufacturers design an imaging

1446
00:56:00,160 --> 00:56:04,240
system, they often advertise its

1447
00:56:01,839 --> 00:56:06,319
specifications, resolution, noise

1448
00:56:04,240 --> 00:56:09,040
levels, dose reduction, acquisition

1449
00:56:06,319 --> 00:56:10,640
speed, and so on. These system specs are

1450
00:56:09,040 --> 00:56:12,400
useful, but in practice, they don't

1451
00:56:10,640 --> 00:56:14,799
directly answer the most important

1452
00:56:12,400 --> 00:56:18,799
question. Can the system help us make

1453
00:56:14,799 --> 00:56:20,799
the right clinical decision?

1454
00:56:18,799 --> 00:56:22,720
Think about it.

1455
00:56:20,799 --> 00:56:25,440
As patients or physicians, we don't

1456
00:56:22,720 --> 00:56:27,680
actually care whether an image has 0.5

1457
00:56:25,440 --> 00:56:29,920
millimeter resolution or 1 millimeter

1458
00:56:27,680 --> 00:56:33,200
resolution or whether the noise level is

1459
00:56:29,920 --> 00:56:35,119
2% or 5%. What we really care about is

1460
00:56:33,200 --> 00:56:37,440
whether the image allows us to detect a

1461
00:56:35,119 --> 00:56:40,079
tumor, diagnose a fracture, or rule out

1462
00:56:37,440 --> 00:56:41,920
disease with confidence.

1463
00:56:40,079 --> 00:56:44,480
That's why taskbased assessment is so

1464
00:56:41,920 --> 00:56:46,000
important. It focuses on the end goal,

1465
00:56:44,480 --> 00:56:50,400
whether the imaging system can

1466
00:56:46,000 --> 00:56:52,079
successfully support the clinical task.

1467
00:56:50,400 --> 00:56:54,240
So in this last part of the lecture

1468
00:56:52,079 --> 00:56:57,200
we'll discuss task specific measures

1469
00:56:54,240 --> 00:57:00,079
such as sensitivity and specificity ROC

1470
00:56:57,200 --> 00:57:01,920
and AU analysis human and mathematical

1471
00:57:00,079 --> 00:57:05,200
observers and even modern approaches

1472
00:57:01,920 --> 00:57:07,200
like neural networks and radiomics.

1473
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:09,280
These measures shift our attention from

1474
00:57:07,200 --> 00:57:11,280
the imaging device itself to the

1475
00:57:09,280 --> 00:57:13,280
diagnostic performance of the entire

1476
00:57:11,280 --> 00:57:16,799
workflow which is ultimately what

1477
00:57:13,280 --> 00:57:19,440
matters most in medical imaging.

1478
00:57:16,799 --> 00:57:21,839
Here

1479
00:57:19,440 --> 00:57:23,680
we see a typical scene from a radiology

1480
00:57:21,839 --> 00:57:25,839
department.

1481
00:57:23,680 --> 00:57:28,160
A radiologist with years of training and

1482
00:57:25,839 --> 00:57:31,040
experience carefully reviews CT scans

1483
00:57:28,160 --> 00:57:33,839
and X-rays. Their task is not to admire

1484
00:57:31,040 --> 00:57:35,839
image sharpness or pixel resolution. It

1485
00:57:33,839 --> 00:57:38,160
is to answer very specific clinical

1486
00:57:35,839 --> 00:57:40,720
questions. Does the smoker have a lung

1487
00:57:38,160 --> 00:57:43,920
tumor?

1488
00:57:40,720 --> 00:57:45,599
Are the coronary arteries narrowed?

1489
00:57:43,920 --> 00:57:47,839
Is there evidence of disease that

1490
00:57:45,599 --> 00:57:49,760
requires treatment?

1491
00:57:47,839 --> 00:57:52,000
This highlights why task specific

1492
00:57:49,760 --> 00:57:53,599
measures are so important. System

1493
00:57:52,000 --> 00:57:56,400
specific measures like spatial

1494
00:57:53,599 --> 00:57:58,319
resolution, SNR or temporal resolution

1495
00:57:56,400 --> 00:58:00,319
are useful, but they are not the end

1496
00:57:58,319 --> 00:58:01,920
goal. They only tell us about the

1497
00:58:00,319 --> 00:58:04,160
technical performance of the imaging

1498
00:58:01,920 --> 00:58:05,839
system. What really matters is whether

1499
00:58:04,160 --> 00:58:08,240
the system can support the clinical

1500
00:58:05,839 --> 00:58:11,520
task, detecting, diagnosing, and guiding

1501
00:58:08,240 --> 00:58:13,119
treatment decisions.

1502
00:58:11,520 --> 00:58:15,200
So in this part of the lecture we'll

1503
00:58:13,119 --> 00:58:18,079
look at task specific measures such as

1504
00:58:15,200 --> 00:58:20,160
sensitivity specificity ROC curves and

1505
00:58:18,079 --> 00:58:22,480
observer studies which are all designed

1506
00:58:20,160 --> 00:58:25,359
to assess performance in clinical terms

1507
00:58:22,480 --> 00:58:27,440
rather than engineering terms.

1508
00:58:25,359 --> 00:58:29,520
This shift in perspective is critical.

1509
00:58:27,440 --> 00:58:31,920
We move from asking how sharp is this

1510
00:58:29,520 --> 00:58:35,640
image to asking can this image help us

1511
00:58:31,920 --> 00:58:35,640
detect disease reliably.

1512
00:58:37,359 --> 00:58:41,280
Let's make this idea of task specific

1513
00:58:39,440 --> 00:58:43,280
measures more concrete with a simple

1514
00:58:41,280 --> 00:58:45,040
example.

1515
00:58:43,280 --> 00:58:46,960
Imagine you are doing edge detection on

1516
00:58:45,040 --> 00:58:49,280
an image. For instance, outlining the

1517
00:58:46,960 --> 00:58:52,000
Eiffel Tower. Each pixel can belong to

1518
00:58:49,280 --> 00:58:54,079
one of two classes, edge or non-edge.

1519
00:58:52,000 --> 00:58:56,319
Similarly, in medical imaging, each case

1520
00:58:54,079 --> 00:58:58,480
can be classified as disease present or

1521
00:58:56,319 --> 00:59:00,880
disease absent.

1522
00:58:58,480 --> 00:59:02,720
This gives us four possible outcomes.

1523
00:59:00,880 --> 00:59:04,559
True positive, the patient has a tumor

1524
00:59:02,720 --> 00:59:06,559
and the system or doctor correctly

1525
00:59:04,559 --> 00:59:08,400
identifies it.

1526
00:59:06,559 --> 00:59:12,000
True negative. The patient is healthy

1527
00:59:08,400 --> 00:59:13,920
and the report correctly says no tumor.

1528
00:59:12,000 --> 00:59:16,240
False positive. The patient is healthy

1529
00:59:13,920 --> 00:59:18,480
but the system says there is a tumor.

1530
00:59:16,240 --> 00:59:20,480
This can cause unnecessary anxiety,

1531
00:59:18,480 --> 00:59:22,400
follow-up scans, or even invasive

1532
00:59:20,480 --> 00:59:24,319
procedures.

1533
00:59:22,400 --> 00:59:26,079
False negative. The patient does have a

1534
00:59:24,319 --> 00:59:28,559
tumor, but the system or doctor misses

1535
00:59:26,079 --> 00:59:30,559
it. This is often the most serious error

1536
00:59:28,559 --> 00:59:32,240
because it can delay diagnosis and

1537
00:59:30,559 --> 00:59:34,400
treatment.

1538
00:59:32,240 --> 00:59:36,799
In clinical practice, both types of

1539
00:59:34,400 --> 00:59:39,040
errors matter. False positives burden

1540
00:59:36,799 --> 00:59:41,119
patients emotionally and financially,

1541
00:59:39,040 --> 00:59:42,640
while false negatives can cost lives by

1542
00:59:41,119 --> 00:59:44,400
missing opportunities for early

1543
00:59:42,640 --> 00:59:46,000
treatment.

1544
00:59:44,400 --> 00:59:48,079
That's why doctors and imaging

1545
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:50,480
researchers often talk about sensitivity

1546
00:59:48,079 --> 00:59:52,559
and specificity.

1547
00:59:50,480 --> 00:59:55,040
Sensitivity reflects how good a system

1548
00:59:52,559 --> 00:59:57,200
is at detecting true positives, catching

1549
00:59:55,040 --> 00:59:59,200
disease when it is really there.

1550
00:59:57,200 --> 01:00:01,680
Specificity reflects how good a system

1551
00:59:59,200 --> 01:00:04,880
is at avoiding false alarms, correctly

1552
01:00:01,680 --> 01:00:06,960
identifying when disease is not present.

1553
01:00:04,880 --> 01:00:08,880
These terms can be confusing at first,

1554
01:00:06,960 --> 01:00:11,599
but they are central to evaluating

1555
01:00:08,880 --> 01:00:13,680
diagnostic imaging systems. They shift

1556
01:00:11,599 --> 01:00:15,920
the focus away from just image sharpness

1557
01:00:13,680 --> 01:00:18,160
or resolution and toward what really

1558
01:00:15,920 --> 01:00:22,599
matters, whether the imaging system can

1559
01:00:18,160 --> 01:00:22,599
support accurate medical decisions.

1560
01:00:24,400 --> 01:00:28,400
Now that we've defined the four possible

1561
01:00:26,480 --> 01:00:30,319
outcomes, true positives, true

1562
01:00:28,400 --> 01:00:32,400
negatives, false positives, and false

1563
01:00:30,319 --> 01:00:35,599
negatives, we can formally define

1564
01:00:32,400 --> 01:00:37,520
sensitivity and specificity.

1565
01:00:35,599 --> 01:00:39,359
Sensitivity is the proportion of true

1566
01:00:37,520 --> 01:00:42,160
positives among all actual positive

1567
01:00:39,359 --> 01:00:46,319
cases. In formula form, it is

1568
01:00:42,160 --> 01:00:48,240
sensitivity equals TP over TP plus FN.

1569
01:00:46,319 --> 01:00:50,400
In other words, sensitivity answers the

1570
01:00:48,240 --> 01:00:54,880
question, if the patient really has the

1571
01:00:50,400 --> 01:00:57,119
disease, how likely are we to detect it?

1572
01:00:54,880 --> 01:00:59,280
For example, suppose 100 patients truly

1573
01:00:57,119 --> 01:01:01,599
have lung cancer.

1574
01:00:59,280 --> 01:01:03,760
If our system correctly detects 50 of

1575
01:01:01,599 --> 01:01:07,119
them, but misses the other 50, then the

1576
01:01:03,760 --> 01:01:08,640
sensitivity is 50%. Sensitivity is

1577
01:01:07,119 --> 01:01:10,799
essentially a measure of how confident

1578
01:01:08,640 --> 01:01:12,480
we are when we say yes, disease is

1579
01:01:10,799 --> 01:01:14,880
present.

1580
01:01:12,480 --> 01:01:16,720
Now, let's move to specificity.

1581
01:01:14,880 --> 01:01:18,720
Specificity is the proportion of true

1582
01:01:16,720 --> 01:01:22,319
negatives among all actual negative

1583
01:01:18,720 --> 01:01:25,359
cases. The formula is specificity equals

1584
01:01:22,319 --> 01:01:27,200
TN over TN plus FP.

1585
01:01:25,359 --> 01:01:28,640
This answers the question, if the

1586
01:01:27,200 --> 01:01:30,799
patient really does not have the

1587
01:01:28,640 --> 01:01:33,839
disease, how likely are we to correctly

1588
01:01:30,799 --> 01:01:36,079
say no?

1589
01:01:33,839 --> 01:01:38,799
For example, if 100 healthy people are

1590
01:01:36,079 --> 01:01:40,720
scanned and 95 are correctly reported as

1591
01:01:38,799 --> 01:01:43,599
negative while five are mistakenly

1592
01:01:40,720 --> 01:01:45,280
labeled as positive, the specificity is

1593
01:01:43,599 --> 01:01:47,280
95%.

1594
01:01:45,280 --> 01:01:49,200
Specificity is essentially a measure of

1595
01:01:47,280 --> 01:01:51,760
how confident we are when we say no,

1596
01:01:49,200 --> 01:01:54,720
there is no disease.

1597
01:01:51,760 --> 01:01:57,920
So in summary, sensitivity tells us how

1598
01:01:54,720 --> 01:01:59,760
good we are at catching disease cases.

1599
01:01:57,920 --> 01:02:02,240
Specificity tells us how good we are at

1600
01:01:59,760 --> 01:02:04,799
avoiding false alarms.

1601
01:02:02,240 --> 01:02:07,040
Both are critical in clinical imaging.

1602
01:02:04,799 --> 01:02:09,680
High sensitivity ensures we don't miss

1603
01:02:07,040 --> 01:02:11,520
disease while high specificity ensures

1604
01:02:09,680 --> 01:02:15,960
we don't overwhelm patients with

1605
01:02:11,520 --> 01:02:15,960
unnecessary worry or treatment.

1606
01:02:17,520 --> 01:02:20,960
Now to make things even more

1607
01:02:18,960 --> 01:02:23,280
interesting, we introduce two more

1608
01:02:20,960 --> 01:02:26,240
important concepts. Positive predictive

1609
01:02:23,280 --> 01:02:30,880
value PPV and negative predictive value

1610
01:02:26,240 --> 01:02:33,200
NPV. Positive predictive value or PPV

1611
01:02:30,880 --> 01:02:35,280
tells us if the imaging study says a

1612
01:02:33,200 --> 01:02:39,200
patient is abnormal, how likely is it

1613
01:02:35,280 --> 01:02:42,480
that they really have the disease?

1614
01:02:39,200 --> 01:02:44,160
The formula is PPV equals true positives

1615
01:02:42,480 --> 01:02:45,839
over true positives plus false

1616
01:02:44,160 --> 01:02:47,760
positives.

1617
01:02:45,839 --> 01:02:50,160
So among all the patients labeled as

1618
01:02:47,760 --> 01:02:52,960
abnormal, PPV is the fraction who

1619
01:02:50,160 --> 01:02:54,799
actually do have the disease.

1620
01:02:52,960 --> 01:02:57,680
On the other hand, negative predictive

1621
01:02:54,799 --> 01:02:59,599
value or NPV tells us if the imaging

1622
01:02:57,680 --> 01:03:01,280
study says a patient is normal, how

1623
01:02:59,599 --> 01:03:04,280
likely is it that they really are

1624
01:03:01,280 --> 01:03:04,280
disease-free?

1625
01:03:04,319 --> 01:03:09,520
The formula is NPV equals true negatives

1626
01:03:07,680 --> 01:03:11,280
over true negatives plus false

1627
01:03:09,520 --> 01:03:13,040
negatives.

1628
01:03:11,280 --> 01:03:15,280
So among all the patients labeled as

1629
01:03:13,040 --> 01:03:17,520
normal, NPV is the fraction who truly

1630
01:03:15,280 --> 01:03:20,400
have no disease.

1631
01:03:17,520 --> 01:03:22,400
Let's put this in clinical terms.

1632
01:03:20,400 --> 01:03:24,799
Suppose we are using CT to detect a

1633
01:03:22,400 --> 01:03:27,200
tumor. If the scan flags a suspicious

1634
01:03:24,799 --> 01:03:29,039
spot, PPV tells us how likely it is that

1635
01:03:27,200 --> 01:03:31,359
this really is a tumor and not just a

1636
01:03:29,039 --> 01:03:33,760
false alarm. If the scan comes back

1637
01:03:31,359 --> 01:03:38,319
clear, NPV tells us how confident we can

1638
01:03:33,760 --> 01:03:40,799
be that the patient truly has no tumor.

1639
01:03:38,319 --> 01:03:42,720
So sensitivity and specificity tell us

1640
01:03:40,799 --> 01:03:45,839
how the test performs in an abstract

1641
01:03:42,720 --> 01:03:47,760
sense relative to ground truth. PPV and

1642
01:03:45,839 --> 01:03:49,359
NPV tell us what the test result

1643
01:03:47,760 --> 01:03:52,839
actually means for the patient sitting

1644
01:03:49,359 --> 01:03:52,839
in front of us.

1645
01:03:54,079 --> 01:03:58,319
Here's a concrete example using

1646
01:03:55,760 --> 01:04:00,799
tuberculosis screening with chest X-ray.

1647
01:03:58,319 --> 01:04:02,799
In this study, nearly 2,000 people were

1648
01:04:00,799 --> 01:04:05,119
tested, but only 30 actually had

1649
01:04:02,799 --> 01:04:07,520
tuberculosis.

1650
01:04:05,119 --> 01:04:10,480
Let's break this down.

1651
01:04:07,520 --> 01:04:12,400
Among the 30 true TB patients, 22 were

1652
01:04:10,480 --> 01:04:14,559
correctly reported as positive while

1653
01:04:12,400 --> 01:04:16,720
eight were missed.

1654
01:04:14,559 --> 01:04:19,359
Among the 1,790

1655
01:04:16,720 --> 01:04:21,520
