01:25:43,120 --> 01:25:48,800
simple yes or no question. For example,

2204
01:25:45,600 --> 01:25:50,960
does this patient have a tumor or not?

2205
01:25:48,800 --> 01:25:53,920
Mathematically, we describe this with

2206
01:25:50,960 --> 01:25:56,159
two competing hypotheses.

2207
01:25:53,920 --> 01:25:58,080
The first hypothesis says the image only

2208
01:25:56,159 --> 01:26:01,360
contains the background. In other words,

2209
01:25:58,080 --> 01:26:03,280
normal anatomy plus noise.

2210
01:26:01,360 --> 01:26:05,120
The second hypothesis says the image

2211
01:26:03,280 --> 01:26:07,520
contains both the background and an

2212
01:26:05,120 --> 01:26:09,920
additional signal such as a tumor, again

2213
01:26:07,520 --> 01:26:11,600
with noise added.

2214
01:26:09,920 --> 01:26:13,840
Here we think of the background as the

2215
01:26:11,600 --> 01:26:16,000
normal structures in the body and the

2216
01:26:13,840 --> 01:26:18,239
signal as the diagnostic feature we're

2217
01:26:16,000 --> 01:26:19,920
trying to detect.

2218
01:26:18,239 --> 01:26:21,840
Depending on the situation, we might

2219
01:26:19,920 --> 01:26:24,000
know exactly what that signal looks like

2220
01:26:21,840 --> 01:26:26,639
or only know its general statistical

2221
01:26:24,000 --> 01:26:28,800
properties. For example, in phantom

2222
01:26:26,639 --> 01:26:31,040
studies, we know exactly what pattern is

2223
01:26:28,800 --> 01:26:34,719
inserted. But in real patients, tumors

2224
01:26:31,040 --> 01:26:36,639
can vary in shape, size, and contrast.

2225
01:26:34,719 --> 01:26:38,800
To simplify the discussion, we often

2226
01:26:36,639 --> 01:26:41,120
imagine a clean background image without

2227
01:26:38,800 --> 01:26:42,639
noise and a clean signal image without

2228
01:26:41,120 --> 01:26:44,800
noise.

2229
01:26:42,639 --> 01:26:46,880
The actual measurement we record is just

2230
01:26:44,800 --> 01:26:49,679
those two components combined with noise

2231
01:26:46,880 --> 01:26:52,880
from the imaging process.

2232
01:26:49,679 --> 01:26:54,719
So in essence the classification task is

2233
01:26:52,880 --> 01:26:57,199
given the measured image do we believe

2234
01:26:54,719 --> 01:27:01,520
it came from the background only case or

2235
01:26:57,199 --> 01:27:03,920
from the background plus signal case.

2236
01:27:01,520 --> 01:27:06,480
This very simple framework background

2237
01:27:03,920 --> 01:27:08,719
versus background plus signal forms the

2238
01:27:06,480 --> 01:27:11,719
basis of many observer models in medical

2239
01:27:08,719 --> 01:27:11,719
imaging.

2240
01:27:13,199 --> 01:27:17,040
Now let's introduce the idea of the

2241
01:27:14,960 --> 01:27:19,440
ideal observer.

2242
01:27:17,040 --> 01:27:21,520
In the context of binary classification,

2243
01:27:19,440 --> 01:27:23,360
the ideal observer is defined as the one

2244
01:27:21,520 --> 01:27:25,120
who makes use of all the statistical

2245
01:27:23,360 --> 01:27:27,600
information available in order to

2246
01:27:25,120 --> 01:27:29,840
maximize task performance. In other

2247
01:27:27,600 --> 01:27:31,600
words, it's the absolute gold standard,

2248
01:27:29,840 --> 01:27:34,159
the best decision maker you could have,

2249
01:27:31,600 --> 01:27:36,560
whether human or machine.

2250
01:27:34,159 --> 01:27:38,159
How does it work?

2251
01:27:36,560 --> 01:27:40,719
Imagine you have an image which we'll

2252
01:27:38,159 --> 01:27:43,760
call G. For this image, there are two

2253
01:27:40,719 --> 01:27:45,679
possible explanations. Hypothesis zero

2254
01:27:43,760 --> 01:27:48,000
says it comes from a normal case with no

2255
01:27:45,679 --> 01:27:49,760
abnormality.

2256
01:27:48,000 --> 01:27:51,840
Hypothesis one says it comes from an

2257
01:27:49,760 --> 01:27:53,920
abnormal case where a signal such as a

2258
01:27:51,840 --> 01:27:56,000
tumor is present.

2259
01:27:53,920 --> 01:27:58,480
The ideal observer looks at both

2260
01:27:56,000 --> 01:28:00,480
possibilities and asks given the data I

2261
01:27:58,480 --> 01:28:02,639
see, how likely is it that it came from

2262
01:28:00,480 --> 01:28:06,960
the normal case and how likely is it

2263
01:28:02,639 --> 01:28:09,040
that it came from the abnormal case?

2264
01:28:06,960 --> 01:28:11,440
The decision is then based on whichever

2265
01:28:09,040 --> 01:28:13,520
likelihood is greater.

2266
01:28:11,440 --> 01:28:15,360
If the abnormal case is more likely, the

2267
01:28:13,520 --> 01:28:17,360
observer calls it abnormal. If the

2268
01:28:15,360 --> 01:28:19,520
normal case is more likely, the observer

2269
01:28:17,360 --> 01:28:21,520
calls it normal.

2270
01:28:19,520 --> 01:28:23,679
This simple but powerful rule, often

2271
01:28:21,520 --> 01:28:26,480
called the likelihood ratio test,

2272
01:28:23,679 --> 01:28:28,400
provides an upper bound on performance.

2273
01:28:26,480 --> 01:28:30,400
No other observer, whether it's another

2274
01:28:28,400 --> 01:28:33,040
model or even an expert human reader,

2275
01:28:30,400 --> 01:28:34,880
can systematically do better.

2276
01:28:33,040 --> 01:28:37,280
That's why the ideal observer always

2277
01:28:34,880 --> 01:28:39,760
produces the highest possible ROC curve

2278
01:28:37,280 --> 01:28:42,159
for the task. It gives us a theoretical

2279
01:28:39,760 --> 01:28:44,239
benchmark, a way to measure how close or

2280
01:28:42,159 --> 01:28:47,719
how far real systems and real doctors

2281
01:28:44,239 --> 01:28:47,719
are from perfection.

2282
01:28:49,360 --> 01:28:53,199
The ideal observer, as we just

2283
01:28:51,199 --> 01:28:55,199
discussed, requires complete statistical

2284
01:28:53,199 --> 01:28:57,040
knowledge of the problem. But in

2285
01:28:55,199 --> 01:28:59,280
practice, we almost never have that

2286
01:28:57,040 --> 01:29:01,679
luxury. So instead, we turn to

2287
01:28:59,280 --> 01:29:03,120
simplified models.

2288
01:29:01,679 --> 01:29:05,679
One of the most important of these is

2289
01:29:03,120 --> 01:29:08,159
the hoteling observer.

2290
01:29:05,679 --> 01:29:10,320
The idea is straightforward. Rather than

2291
01:29:08,159 --> 01:29:12,639
using all the complex statistics of the

2292
01:29:10,320 --> 01:29:15,280
image, we take the image data and apply

2293
01:29:12,639 --> 01:29:17,440
a linear template to it. In other words,

2294
01:29:15,280 --> 01:29:20,320
we reduce the entire image into a single

2295
01:29:17,440 --> 01:29:22,080
number, a test statistic. That number is

2296
01:29:20,320 --> 01:29:26,000
then used to decide whether the case is

2297
01:29:22,080 --> 01:29:28,000
more likely normal or abnormal.

2298
01:29:26,000 --> 01:29:30,480
The hoteling observer is designed to be

2299
01:29:28,000 --> 01:29:32,800
the best possible linear observer. What

2300
01:29:30,480 --> 01:29:35,120
does best mean here? It means the

2301
01:29:32,800 --> 01:29:37,520
observer chooses its template in a way

2302
01:29:35,120 --> 01:29:39,679
that maximizes the signal to noise

2303
01:29:37,520 --> 01:29:42,960
ratio, the separation between the

2304
01:29:39,679 --> 01:29:44,880
diseased and nondesed cases. The larger

2305
01:29:42,960 --> 01:29:46,719
the separation, the easier the decision

2306
01:29:44,880 --> 01:29:48,480
becomes.

2307
01:29:46,719 --> 01:29:50,719
This is why the hoteling observer is so

2308
01:29:48,480 --> 01:29:52,800
widely studied.

2309
01:29:50,719 --> 01:29:54,320
It strikes a balance, not as powerful as

2310
01:29:52,800 --> 01:29:56,239
the ideal observer, but still

2311
01:29:54,320 --> 01:29:58,880
mathematically optimal within the class

2312
01:29:56,239 --> 01:30:00,719
of linear decision rules.

2313
01:29:58,880 --> 01:30:03,199
And if we don't even want to use all the

2314
01:30:00,719 --> 01:30:05,199
image data, we can go a step further. We

2315
01:30:03,199 --> 01:30:06,960
might only use selected features or

2316
01:30:05,199 --> 01:30:09,520
specific channels of the image instead

2317
01:30:06,960 --> 01:30:11,760
of the full data set.

2318
01:30:09,520 --> 01:30:14,239
That gives us the channelized hoteling

2319
01:30:11,760 --> 01:30:16,639
observer, a practical simplification

2320
01:30:14,239 --> 01:30:20,520
that still works well in many real world

2321
01:30:16,639 --> 01:30:20,520
medical imaging problems.

2322
01:30:22,159 --> 01:30:26,480
Now let's talk about what is called the

2323
01:30:23,840 --> 01:30:28,480
channelized observer. The idea here is

2324
01:30:26,480 --> 01:30:31,520
to simplify the problem of analyzing

2325
01:30:28,480 --> 01:30:33,120
very highdimensional image data.

2326
01:30:31,520 --> 01:30:35,199
Instead of working with every single

2327
01:30:33,120 --> 01:30:37,120
pixel value, which can be overwhelming,

2328
01:30:35,199 --> 01:30:38,960
we break the image down into a set of

2329
01:30:37,120 --> 01:30:41,120
channels.

2330
01:30:38,960 --> 01:30:42,719
Each channel acts like a filter or a

2331
01:30:41,120 --> 01:30:44,960
template that extracts a particular

2332
01:30:42,719 --> 01:30:47,040
aspect of the image. Think of them as

2333
01:30:44,960 --> 01:30:48,719
different views or features of the same

2334
01:30:47,040 --> 01:30:50,480
data.

2335
01:30:48,719 --> 01:30:52,320
When we apply these channels to the

2336
01:30:50,480 --> 01:30:54,960
image, we get a series of scalar

2337
01:30:52,320 --> 01:30:57,760
responses, simple numbers that summarize

2338
01:30:54,960 --> 01:30:59,840
how the image looks under each channel.

2339
01:30:57,760 --> 01:31:01,760
We then collect these responses into a

2340
01:30:59,840 --> 01:31:03,600
much smaller vector, which is far easier

2341
01:31:01,760 --> 01:31:05,679
to work with than the original full

2342
01:31:03,600 --> 01:31:07,760
image data.

2343
01:31:05,679 --> 01:31:10,239
This is what's known as a channelized

2344
01:31:07,760 --> 01:31:11,760
observer. Reducing the dimensionality

2345
01:31:10,239 --> 01:31:13,920
allows us to perform statistical

2346
01:31:11,760 --> 01:31:15,600
analysis more efficiently while still

2347
01:31:13,920 --> 01:31:17,920
retaining the essential information

2348
01:31:15,600 --> 01:31:19,760
needed to judge image quality or detect

2349
01:31:17,920 --> 01:31:22,159
a signal.

2350
01:31:19,760 --> 01:31:24,239
So in short, channelize observers strike

2351
01:31:22,159 --> 01:31:26,800
a balance. They simplify the

2352
01:31:24,239 --> 01:31:31,080
computational task but still capture the

2353
01:31:26,800 --> 01:31:31,080
critical diagnostic features.

2354
01:31:32,560 --> 01:31:36,159
To make the idea of channelized

2355
01:31:34,159 --> 01:31:39,760
observers more concrete, let me give you

2356
01:31:36,159 --> 01:31:41,760
an example. Here you see four channels

2357
01:31:39,760 --> 01:31:44,000
illustrated both in the frequency domain

2358
01:31:41,760 --> 01:31:47,360
across the top row and in the spatial

2359
01:31:44,000 --> 01:31:49,520
domain across the bottom row. Each

2360
01:31:47,360 --> 01:31:51,360
channel acts like a filter. In the

2361
01:31:49,520 --> 01:31:53,199
frequency domain, these filters look

2362
01:31:51,360 --> 01:31:55,760
like concentric rings that select

2363
01:31:53,199 --> 01:31:57,600
specific frequency ranges. When we

2364
01:31:55,760 --> 01:31:59,440
transform them back into the spatial

2365
01:31:57,600 --> 01:32:02,080
domain, they appear as blurred or

2366
01:31:59,440 --> 01:32:05,280
oscilly patterns.

2367
01:32:02,080 --> 01:32:07,120
Why do we do this?

2368
01:32:05,280 --> 01:32:08,960
Because instead of trying to analyze the

2369
01:32:07,120 --> 01:32:11,679
entire frequency spectrum all at once,

2370
01:32:08,960 --> 01:32:13,600
we divide it into pieces. Each channel

2371
01:32:11,679 --> 01:32:15,520
captures information from one part of

2372
01:32:13,600 --> 01:32:17,280
the spectrum. When we apply these

2373
01:32:15,520 --> 01:32:19,520
filters to an image, we can see how

2374
01:32:17,280 --> 01:32:21,920
strongly the image responds to each band

2375
01:32:19,520 --> 01:32:23,840
of frequencies.

2376
01:32:21,920 --> 01:32:25,679
This is very powerful because medical

2377
01:32:23,840 --> 01:32:28,080
images contain structures at many

2378
01:32:25,679 --> 01:32:31,120
different scales. Some large and smooth,

2379
01:32:28,080 --> 01:32:33,199
others fine and detailed. Using channels

2380
01:32:31,120 --> 01:32:35,760
allows us to separate these scales and

2381
01:32:33,199 --> 01:32:37,520
study them systematically.

2382
01:32:35,760 --> 01:32:39,120
So these four channels are just one

2383
01:32:37,520 --> 01:32:40,800
example, but in practice you could

2384
01:32:39,120 --> 01:32:44,920
design more channels depending on how

2385
01:32:40,800 --> 01:32:44,920
much detail you want to capture.

2386
01:32:46,400 --> 01:32:50,480
Now let's move into the topic of

2387
01:32:48,000 --> 01:32:53,040
radomics which is an exciting area in

2388
01:32:50,480 --> 01:32:55,040
modern medical imaging.

2389
01:32:53,040 --> 01:32:57,600
Radiomics is all about turning images

2390
01:32:55,040 --> 01:33:00,159
into data. Instead of relying only on

